1380-  A signed graph (G, Σ) is an undirected graph G together with an assignment
1381-  of signs (positive or negative) to all its edges, where Σ denotes the set of
1382-  negative edges. Two signatures are said to be equivalent if one can be
1383-  obtained from the other by a sequence of resignings (i.e. switching the sign
1384-  of all edges incident to a given vertex). Extending the notion of usual graph
1385-  homomorphisms, homomorphisms of signed graphs were introduced, and have lead
1386-  to some extensions and strengthenings in the theory of graph colorings and
1387:  homomorphisms. We study the complexity of deciding whether a given signed
1388-  graph admits a homomorphism to a fixed target signed graph [H,Σ], i.e. the
1389-  (H,Σ)-Coloring problem. We prove a dichotomy result for the class of all (C
1390-  k ,Σ)-Coloring problems (where C k is a cycle of length k ≥ 3): (C k
1391-  ,Σ)-Coloring is NP-complete, unless both k and the size of Σ are even. We
1392-  conjecture that this dichotomy can be extended to all signed graphs in a
1393-  natural way. We also introduce the more general concept of signed constraint
1394-  satisfaction problems and show that a dichotomy for such problems is
1395-  equivalent to the statement of the Feder-Vardi Dichotomy Conjecture.%
