+# arXiv 1608.06944 — zero-sum 3-flow generator
+
+Status: the generator and every local gate pass, but the required cross-vendor oracle run is **not complete**. OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 `key limit exceeded` on every bare, structural-hint, and placebo request. The error-only transcripts are preserved; they are not counted as model failures. Re-run the harness after the key limit is restored before treating this family as shipped.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **A — structural hardness** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (the paper's edge-flow vector) |
+| Native objects | all-negative signed simple `(3,4)`-semiregular graph, its 0/1 incidence matrix, integer zero-sum 3-flow |
+| Intended intuition | constraint propagation: couple each degree-3 vertex's sign type and magnitude-two edge at the degree-4 vertices |
+| Domain essentiality | native; no reduction |
+
+## Problem and provenance
+
+The family comes from Kaiser, Lukot'ka and Rollová, [*Nowhere-zero flows in signed graphs: A survey*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.06944). Section 2.7 defines a zero-sum `k`-flow as a nonzero integer edge labelling whose incident values sum to zero at every vertex, and identifies it with a nowhere-zero flow of the all-negative signed graph. The solver receives such a signed graph and must return one value from `{-2,-1,1,2}` for every edge. `verify` checks the alphabet, the forced multiplicities, and every incidence sum using exact integers.
+
+Generation is inverse, never search-based. It first samples `2n` degree-3 patterns `(1,1,-2)` and `2n` opposite patterns `(-1,-1,2)`. On the degree-4 side, `n` patterns are `(1,1,-1,-1)` and `2n` contain one of each value. Equal-valued half-edges are joined by random simple matchings, after which vertices and edges are shuffled. The sampled edge values are the certificate.
+
+## Why Track A
+
+Theorem 27(ii) in Section 2.7 reports NP-completeness of zero-sum 3-flow on `(3,4)`-semiregular graphs. The easy regime is equally important: Theorem 27(i) gives a polynomial algorithm for `(2,4)`-graphs having only `O(log N)` degree-4 vertices. Shipping `n=18` instead has 72 degree-3 and 54 degree-4 vertices, so the degree-4 population is linear. Worst-case NP-completeness alone does not certify the planted distribution: the empirical evidence is a construction-aware panel on eight seeds. A specialized six-state GAC/DPLL search failed all eight after 500 nodes per seed, averaging 6,848,770 exact constraint-table checks and 10.98 seconds per instance in the recorded run. No efficient algorithm is known for this generated distribution, but the caveats below matter.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=1, seed=1)`, the complete edge list is:
+
+```text
+0:1-3  1:1-6  2:0-6  3:4-5  4:0-3  5:2-6
+6:2-4  7:2-3  8:5-6  9:3-5  10:1-4  11:0-4
+```
+
+One answer is `[-1,2,-2,-2,1,-1,2,-1,1,1,-1,1]`. It gives `verify(inst, inst["answer"]) == (True, "ok")`; replacing its first entry by zero gives `(False, "edge 0 value 0 is outside {-2,-1,1,2}")`. This demo is hand-scale: a person can group one magnitude-two edge with two opposite unit values at each degree-3 vertex and check the three degree-4 sums. It has 90 valid flows among 486 candidates that already satisfy every degree-3 equation.
+
+## Difficulty
+
+| Preset | `n` | vertices | edges / answer atoms | compact JSON chars | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 1 | 7 | 12 | 31 | hand example; not hardened |
+| easy | 18 | 126 | 216 | 541 | nominal shipping preset; local gates pass, oracle pending |
+| medium | 19 | 133 | 228 | 571 | reserve escalation |
+| hard | 20 | 140 | 240 | 601 | reserve escalation; close to the 256-atom cap |
+
+Further growth is `cap_bound`: every extra unit adds twelve mandatory edge values, and `n=22` would exceed the answer cap. There is no honest fixed-answer-length dial for a witness that assigns every edge.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted certificates verify across all presets |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | tagged fenced JSON round-trips and verifies |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 hits from the structure-aware degree-3-satisfying language; space size `9,969,099,311,002,567,785,128,210,212,301,880,871,969,283,458,216,957,764` |
+| G5 | sampled shipping density `0.0`; strongest attack averaged 501 visited nodes, 6,848,770 table checks and 10.98 s |
+| G6 | four-cycle outlier, two residual-greedy orders, 32-restart swap descent, two-stage type/selector, and exact GAC/DPLL all scored 0/8 |
+| G7 | doubled instance (`n=36`, 432 edges) builds and verifies |
+| G8 | 40/40 single/composed relabellings preserve the key and carried witness; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 541 chars, 136 estimated tokens, 216 atoms, 216 post-search assignments; all within caps |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+The harness produced service-error records only, so there is no solved/unsolved oracle attempt and no hardness verdict.
+
+| Arm | Preset | Seeds requested | Solved / valid attempts | Service errors | Conclusion |
+|---|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 1690692091, 2024976602, 876598007, 2034521126 | 0/0 | 4 | unavailable; rerun required |
+| structural | easy | 2084852478, 1753323719, 1317177709, 2090057528 | 0/0 | 4 | unavailable |
+| placebo | easy | 937343249, 1203185552, 1737852503, 2038327095 | 0/0 | 4 | unavailable |
+
+`hinted − placebo` is therefore undefined, not zero. The structural hint has not yet been tested against an oracle.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from results.1608.06944 import gen_1608_06944 as g  # use importlib if dotted numeric paths are unsupported
+
+inst = g.make_instance(n=1, seed=1)
+statement = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, emit after a successful oracle rerun with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1608.06944 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+- The NP-completeness theorem is worst-case; it does not prove this planted distribution hard. The five attacks are evidence, not a reduction-based average-case theorem. A modern external SAT/CP/ILP solver with clause learning was not available under the dependency rule and remains the most important untried attack.
+- Zero hits in 200,000 samples is an observed fraction, not a proof that the true density is zero (or even a tight confidence bound below `1e-6`). The sampler is nevertheless stringent: it enforces global sign-type balance and every degree-3 conservation equation before testing degree-4 vertices.
+- The canonical key is an individualized color-refinement/BFS invariant, not a complete graph-isomorphism canonical form. It passed the required relabellings and diversity tests but could collide on specially constructed nonisomorphic graphs.
+- Every signature is all-negative. This is native coverage because Section 2.7 explicitly identifies zero-sum flows with that signed-graph case, but the family does not exercise mixed signs, switching, or general bidirections from the rest of the survey.
+- The answer has 216 entries. It is below the formal cap, but transcription mistakes remain a practical risk. Most importantly, the four-vendor oracle and G9 comparisons are still unmeasured because the supplied OpenRouter key hit its total limit.
